# Deploy = GitHub Pages (not AWS)

**Production is still GitHub Pages.** AWS is local/dev tooling and optional cloud experiments — it is **not** the production cutover yet.

## What “deploy” means

When the user says **deploy**, **publish**, **ship**, or **push to prod** (without naming AWS), they mean the **Hub static site → GitHub Pages**:

- `npm run publish:hub` (build web + next-step hint)
- `npm run deploy:hub` (`git subtree push --prefix=rayenz-hub hub-prod main`)

Do **not** interpret bare “deploy” as AWS / SAM / `deploy:api`.

## Never run (unless AWS is named)

- `npm run deploy:api` or `npm run deploy:api:guided` or `npm run deploy:cognito`
- `sam deploy` (any variant)
- AWS CLI that creates, updates, or deletes cloud resources (stacks, Lambda, DynamoDB, S3, SSM, Cognito, etc.)
- Offering or suggesting an AWS deploy when the user only said “deploy”

## Allowed without override

- Local API: `npm run start:api`, `sam local start-api`, `sam build`, DynamoDB Local, MinIO
- `npm run test:api` / `npm run test:api:deployed` (when the user supplies `HUB_API_URL` / credentials)
- Docs/code for AWS tooling
- `git push` to GitHub remotes (source repo or Pages subtree via `deploy:hub`)

## AWS override (strict)

Only touch AWS when the user **names AWS or the API script**, e.g. “deploy to AWS”, “run deploy:api”, “deploy:cognito”, “sam deploy”, “push the Lambda stack”.  
Phrases like “deploy”, “deploy it”, “commit push and deploy” are **not** an AWS override.
